1. DEFINITIONS

"Software" means the TigerAI code, configuration, prompts, documentation, and
other material expressly provided under this license.

"Covered Work" means the Software, any modified version or derivative of it,
and any combined software application incorporating or linking to the Software
as a component. Independent programs merely distributed alongside a Covered
Work, or communicating with it solely through an ordinary network protocol,
are not Covered Works solely for that reason.

"Corresponding Source" means the complete, machine-readable preferred form
for modifying the Covered Work, including your modifications, incorporated
application code, prompt and rule configuration, interface definitions, and
scripts and instructions needed to build, install, and run that version.
Minified bundles or compiled output alone do not qualify. Encoded configuration
qualifies only if the source includes tools and instructions to decode, edit,
and re-encode it. General-purpose tools, operating systems, and independently
licensed unmodified dependencies need not be copied into the source, provided
the dependencies and how to obtain them are documented sufficiently to build
and run the Covered Work. Credentials, private keys, user conversations, personal
data, and runtime user data are not required; provide safe example configuration
where needed instead.

"Commercial Use" means any use primarily intended for or directed toward
commercial advantage or monetary compensation. It includes selling or charging
for the Covered Work or access to it; using it in paid, advertising-supported,
sponsored, or other revenue-generating products or services; and using it to
operate or support a commercial business, including internal business use.
Commercial Use remains commercial even if the Covered Work itself is free of
charge, its source is shared, or the operator is a nonprofit organization.

"Noncommercial Use" means use that is not Commercial Use. Personal study,
private entertainment, hobby projects, and educational or academic research
qualify only when they are not directed toward a commercial application or
advantage. Paying an independent hosting or AI API provider for resources does
not, by itself, make an otherwise noncommercial activity commercial.

